PUTC(3)                        P32W Unix                         PUTC(3)

NAME
       putc, fputc, putchar, fputs, puts - write characters or strings

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       int putc(int c, FILE *f);
       int fputc(int c, FILE *f);
       int putchar(int c);
       int fputs(const char *s, FILE *f);
       int puts(const char *s);

DESCRIPTION
       fputc writes the character c, converted to unsigned char, to
       the stream f. putc is the same function (not a macro);
       putchar(c) is putc(c, stdout).

       fputs writes the string s to f, without its NUL and without
       adding a newline. puts writes s and then a newline to stdout.

       Output is buffered as stdio(3) describes: written when the
       buffer is full, at a newline on a line-buffered stream (stdout
       on a terminal), at once on stderr, and by fflush(3), fclose
       and exit(3).

RETURN VALUE
       fputc, putc and putchar return the character written (0 to
       255), or EOF on an error. fputs and puts return 0, or EOF on
       an error.

ERRORS
       Writing to a stream not open for writing, or a failed
       write(2), returns EOF and sets the stream's error flag
       (ferror(3)); after a failed write the buffered output is
       lost.

EXAMPLES
       fputs("name: ", stdout);    /* a prompt, no newline */
       fgets(name, sizeof name, stdin);    /* flushes it first */

SEE ALSO
       write(2), stdio(3), getc(3), printf(3), fread(3)

BUGS
       Each character is a function call; on stderr each is also a
       write(2) system call, so long messages to stderr are slow.
